message.content:
{
  "claim": "The unique polynomial P in Q[x] satisfying P(0)=0 and P'(x)=x^3 - x is P(x) = (1/4)x^4 - (1/2)x^2.",
  "assumptions_or_dependencies": [
    "Standard antiderivative rules over Q[x]: the derivative of x^(n+1)/(n+1) is x^n for n >= 0.",
    "A polynomial's derivative is identically zero iff the polynomial is a constant (constant term only)."
  ],
  "argument": [
    "Antidifferentiate term by term: an antiderivative of x^3 is (1/4)x^4, and an antiderivative of -x is -(1/2)x^2.",
    "Hence every polynomial with P'(x) = x^3 - x has the form P(x) = (1/4)x^4 - (1/2)x^2 + C for some constant C in Q, since the difference of any two antiderivatives has zero derivative and must be constant.",
    "The initial condition P(0) = 0 gives (1/4)*0 - (1/2)*0 + C = 0, so C = 0.",
    "Therefore P(x) = (1/4)x^4 - (1/2)x^2, which has rational coefficients and lies in Q[x].",
    "Check: P(0) = 0 - 0 = 0, and P'(x) = 4*(1/4)x^3 - 2*(1/2)x = x^3 - x, as required.",
    "Uniqueness: any other solution differs by a constant, and the constant is forced to 0 by P(0) = 0, so the answer is unique."
  ],
  "unresolved_obligations": [
    "None; the solution is fully verified by direct differentiation and evaluation at 0."
  ],
  "certificate": {
    "coefficients": ["0", "0", "-1/2", "0", "1/4"]
  }
}

